  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "The asymptotes of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{20}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}=1$ are$\\,\\,y=\\pm $\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The standard equation of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nFor a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin, the equations of the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\na=\\sqrt{20},\\,b=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{20}}{\\sqrt{2}}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{10}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
